# ============================ PAYROLL 6 ============================
LESSONS_P6 = [
L("Chapter 1 — Pay changes: decisions executed as records", "11", "<p>This module governs pay changes: who decides them, how they enter the system, how late decisions pay their arrears, how the annual round runs as a batch, and how the movement record keeps every change explained. The machinery is module 1's — the new-assignment rule; this module builds the governance around it.</p><p><b>The frame.</b> A pay change has two halves, and the custody split (module 1) assigns each:</p><p>1. <b>The decision</b> — whether, whom, how much, from when. This belongs to management: the owner, the approval matrix (Chapter 2). The payroll operator holds no vote and gives no opinion on amounts; what the operator holds is the <b>data</b> the deciders need (Chapter 6's reads) and the <b>refusal</b> that keeps the lane honest: no approved decision, no record.<br>2. <b>The execution</b> — the decision entered as records that compute it: the new Salary Structure Assignment (Chapter 3), the arrears document where the decision came late (Chapter 4), the preview, the run. This is the operator's craft, and its standard is exactness: the record says what the decision said — amount, date, person — nothing more, nothing rounded, nothing \"while we're at it\".</p><p><b>Why governance earns a module.</b> Pay changes are where payroll's discipline is tested by pressure, because every change has an interested party and a deadline:</p><p>• The undocumented rise — promised in a corridor, entered from a message — is the classic dispute seed: two people remember two amounts.<br>• The backdated decision handled casually — history edited, or arrears guessed — breaks the register's honesty in both directions.<br>• The unexplained movement — a base that changed with no decision on file — is the register comparison's most serious finding, because it is either an error or the thing errors are blamed on.</p><p>The module's design makes every change carry its decision like every posting carries its document — the same document-rule, applied to the most sensitive numbers in the business.</p><p><b>The change kinds, previewed.</b> The rise (base up, same package) — Chapter 3. The late decision (effective before it was entered) — Chapter 4's arrears. The promotion (role and package change together) — Chapter 5. The annual round (many decisions, one batch) — Chapter 5. The structure-level change (the package itself redesigned) — Chapter 7's boundary with module 8.</p><p><b>Module contents.</b> Chapter 2: the approval matrix. Chapter 3: executing the single change. Chapter 4: arrears. Chapter 5: promotions and the annual round. Chapter 6: the movement record and the deciders' reads. Chapter 7: the boundaries. Chapter 8: the HealthTrade review. Chapter 9: review and the law.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — The approval matrix", "12", "<p>Before any change enters, the business writes who may decide what. The matrix is one page, and the operator enforces it by routing — the same enforcement pattern as every policy in the track.</p><p><b>The matrix's rows.</b> Per change kind, the deciding authority and the evidence the operator requires:</p><p>1. <b>Individual rise inside the annual budget</b> — the approving manager per the business's ladder (branch manager proposes, owner approves is the small-business standard), evidenced by the signed increment letter or the approval on the review record.<br>2. <b>Off-cycle rise</b> (outside the round) — a higher bar by design: the owner's approval regardless of amount, because off-cycle changes are where exceptions breed. The matrix says so plainly, and the operator routes accordingly.<br>3. <b>Promotion</b> — the role decision (HR/owner) plus its pay decision, both evidenced (Chapter 5).<br>4. <b>The annual round</b> — the batch's total approved by the owner against the budget line, then each name per the ladder (Chapter 5).<br>5. <b>Structure redesign</b> — the owner and the accountant together, because the B/H/T split moves statutory amounts (module 2's dependency, resurfacing).</p><p><b>The evidence standard.</b> Every decision reaching the operator carries: <b>who</b> (the named employee), <b>what</b> (the new base, or the new structure and base), <b>from when</b> (the effective date, explicit), and <b>whose approval</b> (the signature or the recorded approval per the matrix). A message that says \"increase Tunde's pay\" fails on three of four counts, and the operator's reply is the standard, not the execution: <i>send the letter with the amount and effective date, and it enters same day.</i> The refusal is procedural, kind, and absolute — the operator who executes vague instructions inherits their disputes.</p><p><b>The letter as the anchor document.</b> The increment or promotion letter — addressed to the employee, stating the new terms and the effective date, signed per the matrix — is the change's anchor: it is what the employee agreed to, what the assignment must match, and what settles every future \"but I was told\" in one look. The operator files it attached to the assignment it produces (Chapter 3), which closes the loop: every assignment traceable to its letter, every letter to its assignment.</p><p><b>Confidential handling, restated.</b> Decisions in flight are the most sensitive traffic payroll carries — the round's list before it is announced, the individual rise before its letter. Module 4's confidentiality rules apply with the volume turned up: the decisions folder access-controlled, no lists in open channels, and the operator's conduct rule (payroll knowledge is never social currency) at its strictest exactly here, because a leaked round is a trust failure across the whole staff at once.</p><p>Practical rule: the matrix page is agreed once with the owner and re-confirmed at the annual review. Its value is the pressure it absorbs on the operator's behalf: \"the matrix requires the owner's approval for off-cycle changes\" is a sentence that ends arguments the operator should never have to win personally.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — Executing the single change", "12", "<p>The approved decision arrives with its letter. Execution is one procedure, run the same way every time.</p><p><b>The procedure.</b></p><p>1. <b>Verify the decision:</b> the four elements present (who, what, from when, whose approval), the approval matching the matrix, the letter in hand.<br>2. <b>Create the new Salary Structure Assignment:</b> the employee, the structure (unchanged for a plain rise; the new one for a package move), the <b>new Base</b> exactly as the letter states, the <b>From Date</b> exactly the letter's effective date, the Income Tax Slab carried, the letter attached to the record. Module 1's supersession rule executes itself: the new assignment governs from its date; the old one stands as history, untouched.<br>3. <b>Preview:</b> the changed employee's slip previewed (module 1, Chapter 7) — the new base flowing through the percentages, the statutory lines moving with it (pension on the new B/H/T; PAYE at the new taxable level — the change's full consequence visible before it pays). The preview against the letter is the execution's proof: the letter's package arithmetic and the slip agree.<br>4. <b>Log the movement:</b> one line in the movement record (Chapter 6): employee, old base → new base, effective date, letter reference, approver, entered by, entered on.<br>5. <b>The run applies it:</b> the next run whose period contains the From Date computes on the new assignment — no run-side action needed. Where the From Date is mid-month, the period splits correctly by the platform's computation; the preview shows exactly how, and the split's arithmetic joins the payday standing answers for that employee.</p><p><b>The timing cases.</b></p><p>• <b>Decided ahead:</b> the letter dated the 20th, effective the 1st of next month — the assignment enters now with its future From Date, and the change waits correctly. Enter decisions when they arrive; the From Date does the scheduling.<br>• <b>Decided in-month:</b> effective the 1st, entered the 12th, run generated the 21st — inside the same period, the run computes it fully; only the records-close matters (a decision entered after the close waits or takes the deliberate lane, module 3's rule).<br>• <b>Decided late:</b> effective before the current period — the assignment enters with its honest past From Date <b>and</b> the already-paid months' difference becomes arrears: Chapter 4's lane. What never happens: the From Date moved forward \"to keep it simple\". The date is the decision's; simplicity is the arrears lane's job.</p><p><b>The employee's side.</b> The letter told them the terms; the first changed slip shows them computed. Where the change lands mid-month or with arrears, the two-line explanation is prepared before payday (module 4's standing-answer discipline): what the letter said, what this slip shows, why they match. A rise that arrives with a confusing slip wastes the goodwill it bought.</p><p>Practical rule: same-day execution as the service standard — a complete decision entered the day it arrives. The lane's speed is what keeps decisions flowing through it rather than around it, exactly as with the sysadmin track's change lane.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — Arrears: the late decision's lane", "13", "<p>A decision effective in the past — the review that concluded in March effective January, the letter signed after the effective date. The months between were paid on the old base, correctly, by the records as they stood. The difference owed is <b>arrears</b>, and it has a lane.</p><p><b>The two halves of the late change.</b></p><p>1. <b>The go-forward half:</b> the new assignment with its <b>honest past From Date</b> (Chapter 3's rule — the date is the decision's). From the current run onward, pay computes on the new base. The past runs' slips are <b>not</b> reopened: they were correct when computed, they are submitted documents, and the arrears lane exists precisely so history can stand.<br>2. <b>The arrears half:</b> the already-paid months' shortfall, computed and paid as its own document.</p><p><b>The arrears computation.</b> On the standing sheet, per affected employee:</p><p>1. Per month from the effective date to the last paid run: the components as they <b>would have computed</b> on the new base, minus as they <b>did compute</b> — the earnings difference. For percentage structures this is arithmetic: the base delta flowed through the package's rates; a proration month (a January joiner with a January-effective rise) prorates the delta identically.<br>2. Sum the months. The sheet shows its working — months, deltas, total — and files with the letter.<br>3. The statutory consequence rides automatically: arrears are taxable pay in the month paid, and the pension family recomputes on the arrears' B/H/T share — the sheet notes the components' split so the entry (next step) carries it correctly.</p><p><b>The entry.</b> One <b>Additional Salary</b> per employee (module 5's machinery — this is its most important standing use): an <b>Arrears</b> component (Earning-type, taxable, wired to the salary expense family — created once through the governed lane, on the register), the sheet's total, the current payroll date, the sheet and letter attached. Material arrears take the <b>Deduct Full Tax</b> policy line like bonuses do (module 5, Chapter 4) — same logic, same consistency rule. Where the split matters to statutory computation (the pension share of arrears), the entry follows the accountant's ruling on how the arrears components are structured — one line or split lines — decided once, on the page.</p><p><b>The slip and the explanation.</b> The arrears month's slip shows the regular pay on the new base <b>plus</b> the arrears line — a large slip that generates the year's most predictable query. The answer is prepared: the letter (effective January), the sheet (three months' difference, here is the arithmetic), the tax note where the flag was ticked. Shown once, understood permanently.</p><p><b>The movement record's entry.</b> The late change logs like any change (Chapter 3), plus the arrears reference: the sheet, the Additional Salary record, the month paid. The register comparison for the arrears month expects the spike and holds its documents.</p><p>Practical rule: arrears exist because decisions were slow, not because payroll was wrong — and the quarterly movement read (Chapter 6) counts them. A business paying arrears every quarter has a decision-timing problem upstream; the count is how the operator hands that fact to the owner as data instead of complaint.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — Promotions and the annual round", "12", "<p>Two composite changes: the promotion (role and pay together) and the annual round (many changes, one batch).</p><p><b>The promotion.</b> A promotion changes the job and the pay, and the records change in order:</p><p>1. <b>The role half:</b> the <b>Employee Promotion</b> record — the platform's document for the event: employee, promotion date, and the property changes it applies (the new Designation, Department or Branch where they move, and related fields). Submitted, it updates the Employee record's properties from the date and stands as the event's document — the role history readable later as a chain of promotion records rather than a mystery of edited fields.<br>2. <b>The pay half:</b> the new Salary Structure Assignment per Chapter 3 — commonly a <b>structure move</b> (Branch Retail Staff → Management) with the new base, from the same effective date, the promotion letter anchoring both halves.<br>3. <b>The consequence walk:</b> a structure move changes the package's shape — different components, different percentages — so the preview is read with extra care: the new package against the letter, the statutory lines on the new B/H/T, and where the old package carried items the new one does not (an allowance that ends with the old role), the letter must have said so — the operator flags the silent difference back to the decider before entering, because the employee will find it on the slip if nobody found it before.</p><p><b>The annual round.</b> The yearly review executes many decisions at once, and the batch has its own procedure:</p><p>1. <b>The list, approved:</b> the round's schedule — every name, current base, new base, effective date — approved as a total against the budget by the owner, then per name per the matrix. The list is the batch's anchor document.<br>2. <b>The batch entry:</b> the assignments created from the list — individually at small scale, by the governed import at volume (the sysadmin track's six steps; the import's dropped-row risk answered by the next step).<br>3. <b>The batch verification — the list tied both ways:</b> every name on the list has its new assignment (none dropped), and every new assignment this cycle is on the list (none invented). The assignment list filtered by the round's From Date is the read; ten minutes that catches both failure directions.<br>4. <b>Previews by structure plus spot names</b>, the letters issued and attached, the movement record batch-logged.<br>5. <b>The round's first run:</b> Station 3's register comparison expects a broad movement and holds the list — every base delta on the register matching the list's delta, the month's one big explanation already written.</p><p><b>The round's confidentiality window.</b> Between the list's approval and the letters' issue, the round is the business's most sensitive document — Chapter 2's rules at maximum: the list lives in the controlled folder, discussions happen in private, and the operator's answer to every corridor question is the same: <i>letters come from management on the date they announce.</i></p><p>Practical rule: the round runs on a published internal timetable — decisions closed, letters issued, effective date, first changed payday — agreed with the owner so the round lands inside one payroll cycle. A round that straggles across months manufactures arrears (Chapter 4) out of pure scheduling.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — The movement record and the deciders' reads", "12", "<p>Governance needs memory and measurement: the movement record remembers every change; the reads give the deciders their data. Both are the operator's products.</p><p><b>The movement record.</b> One running register — a line per change, from Chapters 3-5's logging steps: date entered, employee, kind (rise / promotion / arrears / round), old base → new base, effective date, letter reference, approver. The record's uses:</p><p>1. <b>The audit answer:</b> any base's history reconstructed in one filter — every value it has held, each with its decision. The assignment list holds the same truth record-by-record (module 1: assignments are never edited, so the chain is complete); the movement record is that chain with its governance columns, readable by a human in order.<br>2. <b>The reconciliation:</b> quarterly, the movement record ties to the assignment list — every new assignment has its movement line and its letter; a naked assignment (no line, no letter) is the register comparison's most serious finding, investigated as module 8's audit chapter directs.<br>3. <b>The arrears count</b> (Chapter 4's quarterly read) and the off-cycle count — both handed to the owner as decision-timing data.</p><p><b>The deciders' reads.</b> The operator prepares these for the review cycle — aggregates and structured data, produced from the system, confidentiality rules riding along:</p><p>1. <b>The pay register:</b> the assignment list by structure — who is on what package at what base (module 1's standing view), the round's working document.<br>2. <b>The consistency read:</b> per structure or role family, the base spread — lowest, highest, median — read as aggregates for the deciders to see outliers and compression before the round, not after a grievance. The read surfaces the question (two people, one role, widely different bases); the <b>answer</b> — history, performance, market — is management's, and the operator presents the data without editorial.<br>3. <b>The cost line:</b> the salary cost by cost center from the register's grouping, and the round's modelled cost — the list's deltas annualised, with the employer statutory riding (the employer pension share moves with every base; a round's true cost is the package cost, not the base cost — the modelling sheet says so with arithmetic).<br>4. <b>The budget tie:</b> where the business runs the budget machinery on its salary accounts (the Accounts track's budget module), the round's modelled cost reads against the budget line before approval — the matrix's row 4 assumes this number exists, and the operator is who produces it.</p><p><b>The boundary, held.</b> Every read above is data for deciders. The operator who starts recommending amounts has left their lane and taken sides in every future dispute; the operator who produces clean spreads, true costs, and honest counts is the review cycle's most useful person and nobody's antagonist. The value is the discipline.</p><p>Practical rule: the reads are produced on the round's timetable, dated, and filed with the round — the deciders decided on <i>this</i> data. When next year's round asks \"what did we know last year\", the file answers.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — Boundaries: structure changes and the special cases", "12", "<p>The module's edges: changes that look like pay changes but run other lanes, and the cases that test the rules.</p><p><b>The structure redesign.</b> Changing the <b>package itself</b> — the B/H/T split, a component added or removed for everyone on a structure — is not a pay change; it is a machinery change, and it runs module 8's change discipline: decided by the owner <b>and</b> the accountant together (the split moves pension and tax — module 2's dependency), built as a new or amended structure, previewed per affected structure, and applied by <b>new assignments</b> for the affected employees from the effective date (the supersession rule at batch scale — the old assignments computed the old package honestly and stand). The boundary matters because a redesign entered as \"just edit the structure\" silently changes history's meaning: the old slips reference a structure that no longer says what it said. Module 8 owns the versioning rules; this chapter's job is routing the case there.</p><p><b>The demotion and the reduction.</b> Pay reductions are legally and personally the heaviest change: they require the employee's agreement to varied terms (the letter is a contract variation, signed by both sides — the owner's process, with advice where needed), and only then execute as records — the new assignment at the reduced base from the agreed date, the movement line noting the kind. The operator's line is unchanged and matters most here: no complete evidenced decision, no record — a reduction entered from an instruction without the agreement is the operator executing a breach.</p><p><b>The temporary change.</b> Acting allowances, temporary responsibility pay, secondment terms — pay that changes <b>for a period</b>. The lane is module 5's: a <b>recurring Additional Salary</b> with honest From/To dates (self-terminating — the acting allowance that outlives the acting is that module's Pattern 3), not a base change that must be remembered and reversed. The base moves only for permanent decisions; the distinction is asked explicitly when the decision arrives: <i>permanent or for a period?</i> — and the answer routes the lane.</p><p><b>The correction masquerading as a change.</b> \"His base was entered wrong at go-live\" is not a pay change — it is an error correction: evidenced against the offer letter (the anchor document proving what the base should have been), corrected by a new assignment from the discovery date <b>plus</b> the arrears lane for the shortfall (or module 3's agreed-recovery lane for the overpayment), and logged as a correction with its cause. The distinction matters for the record's honesty: corrections count in the incident log's cause read; changes count in the movement record. Blending them hides both signals.</p><p><b>The freeze months.</b> The matrix can declare freeze windows — no changes entering during the close or the round's own processing — and the operator holds them like the records close: arrivals during a freeze queue with their letters and enter the day it lifts, From Dates honest, arrears lane covering any gap. The freeze protects the batch's integrity; the honest dates protect the employees'.</p><p>Practical rule: every arriving change gets the routing questions in order — permanent or temporary? individual or structural? decision or correction? — thirty seconds that puts each case on its lane before any record is touched.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Case study: HealthTrade's first annual round", "13", "<p>The governance built, then the round run through it. Every chapter appears.</p><p><b>The matrix and the record (Chapters 2, 6).</b> The one-page matrix is agreed with the MD: branch managers propose, the MD approves; off-cycle changes need the MD regardless; the round approves as a total against the budget line first, names second. The movement record opens with the go-live history back-filled from the offer letters — every current base traceable to its letter from day one. The first quarterly reconciliation runs clean: every assignment has its line and letter.</p><p><b>An off-cycle test (Chapters 2-3).</b> Month two, a branch manager messages: <i>increase Kemi's pay, she's carrying the branch.</i> The operator's reply is the standard: the matrix line quoted, the letter template sent. The MD approves, the letter issues — new base, effective the 1st of the following month — and the execution runs same-day: assignment created, letter attached, preview read against the letter (the pension and PAYE lines moving with the base, shown), movement line logged. Kemi's first changed slip matches her letter to the naira; no query arrives.</p><p><b>The round (Chapter 5).</b> The MD sets the timetable: decisions closed by the 10th, letters the 15th, effective the 1st of the next month, first changed payday inside one cycle. The operator produces the deciders' reads (Chapter 6): the pay register by structure, the consistency spread — which surfaces one genuine outlier: <b>two pharmacists, one role, bases eighteen percent apart</b>, presented as data without editorial; the MD resolves it inside the round — and the modelled cost with the employer pension riding, read against the budget line. Twenty-two rises approve. The batch enters by governed import; the both-ways verification catches nothing missing and one extra — <b>an assignment created in a duplicate import row</b> — cancelled before it computes. Letters issue on the 15th; the confidentiality window holds (one corridor question gets the standing answer).</p><p><b>The arrears case (Chapter 4).</b> Two of the twenty-two were review-committee stragglers: decided in the round but agreed effective <b>two months earlier</b> per their review outcomes. Both run the lane: assignments with honest past From Dates; arrears sheets showing the two months' deltas through the package rates; one <b>Arrears</b> component created through the governed lane and registered; two Additional Salary entries with sheets and letters attached, Deduct Full Tax ticked per the materiality line. The arrears month's register comparison expects both spikes and holds both sheets. Both employees get the prepared walk — letter, sheet, tax note — at delivery; zero disputes.</p><p><b>A promotion (Chapter 5).</b> The Ibadan branch supervisor moves to branch manager: the <b>Employee Promotion</b> record carries the designation change from the date; the assignment moves her from Branch Retail Staff to Management with the new base; the preview walk flags that the Retail structure's meal allowance is absent from Management — <b>the letter had not mentioned it</b>; the operator routes the silent difference back to the MD, who amends the letter (the allowance absorbed into the base) before entry. The slip she receives matches the letter she signed. The quarter's movement read goes to the MD as data: twenty-three changes, two arrears cases — both from decision timing — and the note that next year's round timetable closes decisions ten days earlier.</p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Common mistakes & the sixth law", "11", "<p>The governance layer's recurring failures, then the review list.</p><p><b>Pattern 1 — the corridor rise.</b> Symptom: pay changed from messages and recollections; two people remembering two amounts; the dispute arriving with the first slip. Cause: the evidence standard unenforced (Chapter 2). Fix: the four-element standard (who, what, from when, whose approval) with the letter as anchor, the operator's procedural refusal, and same-day execution keeping the lane faster than the corridor.</p><p><b>Pattern 2 — the moved date.</b> Symptom: effective dates shifted forward \"to keep it simple\"; employees paid less than decided; the letter and the records disagreeing. Cause: arrears treated as too hard (Chapters 3-4). Fix: the date is the decision's, always — the honest From Date plus the arrears lane, whose sheet makes the hard part arithmetic.</p><p><b>Pattern 3 — the reopened month.</b> Symptom: past slips cancelled and recomputed to apply late decisions; the register's history unstable; the payroll tie broken backward. Cause: the two-halves design unknown (Chapter 4). Fix: history stands — the past was computed correctly by the records as they stood; the new assignment goes forward, the arrears document covers the gap.</p><p><b>Pattern 4 — the naked assignment.</b> Symptom: a base changed with no movement line and no letter; discovered by the reconciliation or the comparison; error and mischief indistinguishable. Cause: the logging step skipped (Chapters 3, 6). Fix: the movement line as part of execution, the quarterly assignment-to-record reconciliation, and the naked assignment treated as the serious finding it is.</p><p><b>Pattern 5 — the straggling round.</b> Symptom: the annual review dribbling across months; arrears manufactured by scheduling; the round's cost never modelled against the budget before approval. Cause: no timetable, no batch procedure (Chapter 5). Fix: the published timetable inside one cycle, the list as anchor, the both-ways batch verification, and the modelled cost — employer statutory riding — read against the budget line before names approve.</p><p><b>Pattern 6 — the operator with opinions.</b> Symptom: the operator recommending amounts, arguing cases, taking sides; every dispute finding the operator inside it. Cause: the boundary abandoned (Chapters 1, 6). Fix: data without editorial — spreads, costs, counts, produced clean and dated — and the decisions left with the matrix's owners; the discipline is the value.</p><p><b>The review list.</b> Before the quiz, confirm you can: state the two halves of a pay change and the operator's refusal; hold the approval matrix with the four-element evidence standard and the letter as anchor; execute the single change same-day with its preview and movement line, across the three timing cases; run the arrears lane — honest From Date, the sheet's arithmetic, the Arrears component entry with the tax policy, history standing; execute a promotion with the Employee Promotion record and the silent-difference flag, and run the annual round on its timetable with the both-ways verification; produce the deciders' reads — register, spread, modelled cost, budget tie — without editorial; and route the boundary cases by the three questions.</p><p><b>The sixth law: a pay change is a decision executed as a record — approved on the matrix, anchored to its letter, effective from its honest date, arrears by document, history standing, and every movement explained by the record.</b> Module 7 covers exits: the final slip, the settlement, and the leaver's payroll close.</p>"),
]

